<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.3.1: Creating a Pictorial Representation of Exponential Growth</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01275</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.3.1: Creating a Pictorial Representation of Exponential Growth</md:title>
    <md:uuid>45fd5ef9-d3a4-4218-8bd2-cd61574332d9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><figure><media alt="Bacteria viewed through a microscope."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ea0740cee3bc362e73f50595dd33a7bb8b001eb6"/></media></figure></para>
<para>There are some bacteria in a dish. Every hour, each bacterium splits into 3 bacteria.</para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> This diagram shows a bacterium in hour 0 and then hour 1. Draw what happens in hours 2 and 3. </para>
    <para><figure><media alt="The picture shows 1 bacterium (an irregular polygon with a double outline and light blue inside) for hour 0, then an arrow to the right, then 3 bacteria for hour 1."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3b0c4f4a934fc485c117e8f9697531ab47731d55"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Your answers may vary, but here is an example:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Illustration of bacteria growth."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/320bfc4508c74501060b4ed241af8ef602222ebe"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="895d2b51-784f-48d1-a2e9-1164d1863071"><problem><para>
      <para> How many bacteria are there in hour 2?</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>9</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is: There are 9 bacteria in hour 2. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
